import json
from pathlib import Path
inv=json.loads(Path('output/editorial-source-inventory.json').read_text(encoding='utf8')); rs={r['id']:r for r in inv['resources']}
changes={
'ja-sm-your-own-load':{'whyItMatters':'Use this review when the work exceeds the time or support available. Describe the competing demands and their consequences so your manager can decide what to change.','body.0':'Your workload also needs supervision. Explain which responsibilities compete for time, what cannot be completed to the expected standard and which choices require your manager’s decision. Before delegating, review the receiving employee’s existing responsibilities and agree on what can move, decrease or stop.'},
'ja-sm-who-carries-equity-work':{'body.0':'Equity and access work need defined responsibilities, time and recognition. Do not infer someone’s skill, availability or interest from their identity. Distinguish an optional invitation from an assigned responsibility: an invitation allows a genuine choice, while an assignment needs clear expectations and workable resources.','body.2':'Before asking someone to contribute, define the task, relevant skills, expected time, decision authority and how the work will be recognized. Review what must change in the person’s current workload. Explain whether this is an optional opportunity or an assigned responsibility, and discuss barriers to carrying it out.'},
'ja-sm-backing-your-supervisors':{'body.0':'Focus the conversation on a work decision and the evidence behind it. Do not use participation in this voluntary learning program as a performance measure. When the discussion raises a reporting, accommodation or other formal responsibility, involve the responsible office and follow its documentation requirements. Identify an obstacle you can help the supervisor address.'},
'ja-sm-calibration':{'body.0':'Calibration compares evidence against shared criteria; it does not impose a rating curve or quota. Confirm who has authority to recommend, review and approve the decisions being discussed. Share private information only with people whose responsibilities require it. A group pattern calls for examination of the process, not an unsupported change to an individual rating.','body.5':'Before the next review cycle is finalized, arrange a joint review with the appropriate participants and decision authority. Give supervisors time to explain their evidence before offering your own assessment, so your position does not determine the discussion prematurely.'},
'ja-sm-reading-patterns':{'whyItMatters':'Use group patterns to investigate how a process works while protecting people from identification. Individual concerns still belong with the responsible office.','body.4':'After reviewing the evidence, identify a process change within your authority and explain why it addresses the issue you found. Set a point for reviewing its effects, including unintended consequences and results that did not improve. Route concerns about an individual through Human Resources, the Affirmative Action Officer or labor relations.'},
'ja-sm-fair-oversight':{'body.2':'Document why a case was selected, what was checked, plausible alternative explanations, the conclusion and the next step. Identify who reviewed the reasoning. Arrange a second review for difficult judgments and a sample of other cases, within your office’s procedures. Record interpretation or access problems that may affect the evidence.','body.5':'Compare the number of review flags with confirmed findings and examine access effects alongside program-integrity results. A flag does not establish wrongdoing, and findings about particular conduct do not justify claims about an entire community.'},
'ja-sm-senior-leader-sessions':{'summary':'Help senior leaders examine decisions together in a voluntary learning discussion, then take any proposed action through the responsible organizational channels.','body.0':'Keep the learning session voluntary, with no required personal disclosure or identity-based breakouts. Do not use attendance or contributions to evaluate participants. Invite disagreement about the case and address harmful conduct through the appropriate process; do not promise that every statement is exempt from consequences. Reporting duties under Policies 1436 and 1329 still apply. The session does not authorize organizational decisions.','body.1':'Explain the purpose and participation expectations at the start. Form groups by interest or another approach that does not assign roles by identity. Plan communication access before the session. Do not collect personal reflections as learning evidence. Handle any necessary administrative, access or official decision records under the applicable records procedures.'},
'ja-sm-facilitator-guide':{'body.0':'Keep participation voluntary and do not require personal disclosure. Use job roles in practice rather than asking people to perform an identity or simulate a disability. No participant represents an entire community. Invite respectful disagreement without treating learning contributions as a performance assessment. Supervisors’ and managers’ reporting duties under Policies 1436 and 1329 still apply.','body.4':'Let participants keep their own action plans; do not collect them for evaluation. Before removing shared material or administrative records, confirm the applicable retention requirements with the responsible office. Accommodation requests and scheduling records may have obligations separate from the optional learning activity. Do not present attendance as proof of competence.'},
'ja-sm-first-week':{'body.2':'When someone raises an access or accommodation need, ask what would help with the work and contact the ADA coordinator about the appropriate process. Document an oral request and forward it promptly. Any supporting medical documentation belongs with the authorized accommodation process, not an informal request for a diagnosis. ADA coordinator: ____________'},
'ja-sm-deaf-deafblind-hoh':{'body.2':'For an accommodation request, document what was requested and contact the ADA coordinator promptly. Confirm responsibilities for the decision, any supporting information and recurring arrangements. Discuss how the work can be made accessible without collecting medical documentation yourself. Share accommodation information only with those who need it to carry out their responsibilities.'}
}
rows=[]
for id, fields in changes.items():
 r=rs[id]; cs=[]
 for k, after in fields.items():
  path=[int(x) if x.isdigit() else x for x in k.split('.')]; before=r
  for key in path:before=before[key]
  cs.append({'path':path,'before':before,'after':after,'reason':'Clarify practical reasoning, authorized action and privacy without blanket guarantees, automatic deletion or assumptions about identity.'})
 rows.append({'kind':'resource','target':'resource.'+id,'route':'/library/'+id,'original':r,'changes':cs})
result={'status':'pending_independent_review','sources':[{'url':'https://mn.gov/admin/data-practices/data/rules/records-management/','review':'Read complete official page: official records require preservation and disposal under approved retention schedules. A learning-session preference does not override those requirements.'},{'url':'https://mn.gov/dhs/assets/reasonable-accommodation-policy_tcm1053-646974.pdf','review':'Read complete seven-page published policy, especially supervisor responsibilities and confidentiality. Supporting information may be required through the authorized process; supervisors do not receive medical documentation.'}],'records':rows}
Path('output/supervisor-second-revisions.json').write_text(json.dumps(result,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf8')
print(len(rows),sum(len(r['changes']) for r in rows))
